{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>The Elohim of the Hebrews has called upon us.</b> One may wonder: after Pharaoh answered them, 'Who is YHWH that I should obey His voice? I do not know YHWH' — why did Moses and Aaron go back and say, 'The Elohim of the Hebrews has called upon us'?" "<b>Rather,</b> the matter is that the Holy One, blessed be He, said at first, 'And you shall come, you and the elders of Israel,' etc., 'and you shall say to him, YHWH the Elohim of the Hebrews has met with us.' And here they said to him, 'Thus said YHWH, the Elohim of Israel: Let My people go, that they may hold a feast for Me in the wilderness' — they mentioned for Him the designated Name, as they were commanded. And when Pharaoh answered, 'I do not know YHWH,' they then recalled that Pharaoh did know the name Elohim — that He is the Master of the universe and its Guide — for he mentions it constantly in his words: 'since Elohim has made you know all this.' But he did not know the designated Name. Therefore, after he answered, 'I do not know YHWH,' they went back and said to him, 'The Elohim of the Hebrews has called upon us' — which is El Shaddai. And the language of נקרא is from the root of מקרה (occurrence) and פגע (meeting). Therefore they explained the occurrence: 'lest he strike us with plague,' for the Holy One, blessed be He, said to them in the verse, 'And you shall say to him, YHWH the Elohim of the Hebrews has met with us (נקרה עלינו).' For the occurrence includes what they are now saying: 'lest he strike us' — and there is nothing new in their words, only what is included in the words of the Holy One, blessed be He. Therefore they explained to Him that occurrence. And behold, Pharaoh in his wickedness and his wisdom did not answer their words at all, but dismissed them with other matters and said: 'Why, Moses and Aaron, do you disturb,' etc." "<b>And let us sacrifice to YHWH our Elohim, lest He strike us with plague or with the sword.</b> This verse explains the secret of the sacrifices, that they are an atonement from being struck. And this is what it says: 'lest He strike us with plague' (Habakkuk 3:5) — ״כי לפניו ילך דבר ויצא רשף לרגליו״ — 'for before Him goes pestilence, and fiery bolt goes forth at His feet.' And it says, 'or with the sword' — 'for YHWH is the judge by fire,' that is, by the attribute of justice, 'and by His sword upon all flesh.' And you already know that the Shekhinah is the attribute of judgment, and it is the great consuming fire that consumes the sacrifices. And this is what Scripture says (Leviticus 9:6): ״ותפול אש ה' ותאכל״ — 'and the fire of YHWH fell and consumed,' etc. And this is what is written (Numbers 28:2): ״את קרבני לחמי״ — 'My offering, My food' — and He added the explanation: ״לאשי״ — 'for My fire.' And this is what He mentioned at the beginning of the verse: 'The Elohim of the Hebrews has called upon us,' and afterward said, 'Let us go, let us sacrifice to YHWH our Elohim' — and did not say 'to our Elohim,' for the sacrifice is to Elohim but the intention is toward the designated Name. And to this David, peace be upon him, alluded in his saying (Psalms 50:14): ״זבח לאלהים תודה ושלם לעליון נדריך״ — 'Sacrifice to Elohim a thanksgiving, and pay your vows to the Most High' — he explained that the sacrifice is to Elohim but the intention is to El Elyon. And understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:26.891106607Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}